<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tian Han</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as T'ien Han, Tian Shouchang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian">Tian</a> Han was a Chinese playwright, translator and poet and a leader of left-wing drama, music and film, counted by drama historians among the three founders of Chinese spoken drama. After studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1916 to 1922, he founded the Southern Society in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> in 1932 and wrote plays on workers' struggles and resistance to Japan. In 1934 he wrote the lyrics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-of-the-volunteers">March of the Volunteers</a>, set to music by Nie Er, which was adopted as the provisional national anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1949. Denounced and jailed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, he died in 1968; he was rehabilitated in 1979, and his words were restored to the anthem in 1982.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tian-han.jpg" alt="Tian Han" width="222" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:田汉.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
